The version comes from the installed package's metadata, the one place a
release changes it.  A constant here had to be remembered at each release, and
it was not: it went on saying 0.2 while [[pyproject.toml]] moved on.  A source
tree that is merely on [[sys.path]] has no metadata, and there the version is
unknown, which we say rather than give a number that nothing vouches for.  The
lookup asks for the name, not for this copy of the code, so a source tree on the
path of an environment that has another Mentipy installed reports that one.

<<imports>>=
from importlib import metadata
<<constants>>=
UNKNOWN_VERSION = "unknown"

try:
    __version__ = metadata.version("mentipy")
except metadata.PackageNotFoundError:
    __version__ = UNKNOWN_VERSION
@

[[__all__]] documents the intended public API explicitly.  The environment
helpers live here too because the CLI and PythonTeX workflows both benefit from
one obvious import surface when a course repository needs a local virtual
environment.
The base-directory functions belong on the same surface: the companion
LaTeX package (\cref{chap:sty}) calls [[mentipy.set_base_dir]] through
the package root, and a book that binds decks sets the base directory
from its own preamble without knowing which module owns it.

